  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nDaily drumbeat of doldrums with diurnal doses of dour showers and \nstorms. Outside of any thunderstorm driven winds, winds to remain \nstagnant under the generally weak synoptic forcing with the \ndissipation of the midlevel low. We keep our moist airmass, with \nPWATs around 1.7 - 1.8\", enough to help spark at least \norographically originating storms each day, any valley convection \nwill probably follow after the initial terrain upstarts. \n\nInterestingly Euro depicts a drier airmass working its way in for \nSunday into Monday, especially Knoxville and north. This airmass \noriginates from western Canada, and the GFS matches the progression \nuntil the airmass reaches the Midwest, at which point the two models \ndiverge their solutions. NBM has a local minimum in rain chances on \nthose two days, likely tied to the drier solutions in the overall \nensemble. If rain ends up not occurring at the start of the week \nnext week, we probably gain a couple degrees in temperature in \nresponse.\n\nRegardless, we still have a number of days with scattered daily \nstorm chances. No significant severe weather on the horizon at \nleast, and no significant heat, but still we carry the typical \nsummer heat and typical thunderstorm threats from lightning to gusty \nwinds to heavy rainfall. Urban and low lying flooding remains a \npossibility on most days over the next week thanks to the \ncombination of moisture and week steering flow, but should remain \nisolated given the scattered nature of the storms each day.",
